---
title: Strigidae
---
# Strigidae

Strigidae is the larger of the two owl families, holding around two hundred
species across every continent but Antarctica. The facial disc is round rather
than heart-shaped, and many species carry erectile tufts on the crown that have
nothing to do with hearing. They occupy woodland, tundra, desert and city alike,
and take prey by stillness and a silent approach rather than by speed. Eggs are
laid in a tree cavity, an abandoned stick platform or a bare scrape, and
incubation starts with the first egg, so a brood hatches in sequence and the
eldest chick is far the largest.
